# Sway desktop, ported from the maintainer's Nura (postmarketOS) session.
# One package per line; blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
# Files shipped alongside this list live in uis/sway/files/.
#
# Deliberately NOT here:
#   * workstyle - not packaged in Debian (see uis/sway/README.md).

# compositor + session tools
# The compositor is NOT here: `sway` would install Debian's plain sway, and the
# session runs the wlroots fork built by scripts/build-swayfx.sh
# (compositors/swayfx/) which provides sway, conflicts with it and replaces it.
# It installs the same /usr/bin/sway, so greetd, /etc/sway and the keybinds do
# not change; `scripts/install-swayfx.sh` puts it in the rootfs, and the effects
# in config.d/26-swayfx.conf are what plain sway cannot parse.
#
# sway starts Xwayland by default. Without the package every session logs two
# red lines ("cannot find xwayland binary", "Failed to start Xwayland") and
# X11-only programs (emulators, Wine, some Java/Steam games) cannot run at all;
# Nura ships Xwayland. `xwayland disable` in the sway configuration is the
# alternative, and then this package is not needed.
xwayland
swaybg
swayidle
swaylock
wl-clipboard
# clipboard history: cliphist is the one resident process this session has
# (~2 MB). wl-clipboard alone only knows the current selection, so a history
# needs something watching the selection, which is `wl-paste --watch cliphist
# store` in etc/sway/config.d/27-clipboard.conf; Alt+Ctrl+V lists what it kept.
# The database lives in ~/.cache/cliphist/db and holds everything copied,
# including anything secret that passed through the clipboard.
cliphist

# the on-screen keyboard of tablet mode (etc/sway/config.d/28-tablet.conf):
# wvkbd, 826 kB, cairo and pango only, typing through
# zwp_virtual_keyboard_manager_v1, which libwlroots-0.20 exposes and sway
# creates. `tablet-mode on` starts it when cros_ec_buttons reports
# SW_TABLET_MODE and nothing runs it the rest of the time.
wvkbd

# what /etc/sway/config and the drop-ins actually invoke
waybar
# rofi is the launcher (Search key, $mod+d), the command palette ($mod+Shift+d)
# and the window switcher ($mod+Tab, which sway does not provide), and it is also
# what draws the bar's dmenu popups. That is one picker where the session used to
# have two (tofi for the launcher, fuzzel for the bar): rofi 2.0 speaks Wayland
# natively (layer-shell + zwlr_foreign_toplevel), has no daemon, and measured
# 16.4 MB PSS in drun mode / 15.0 MB in window mode while open, against 40.5 MB
# for a fuzzel popup. See uis/sway/README.md, "The launcher".
rofi
# The browser: what rofi-files sends a PDF and the HTML file to, because it is
# the reader with search, zoom and printing, and its own PDF renderer. It is also
# what the minimal /usr/local/bin/xdg-open opens, so foot's ctrl+click and any CLI
# that shells out to xdg-open (yt-dlp's --print-trailer, a script printing a URL)
# reach a browser instead of failing. 280 MB installed is the largest single
# addition in this list, and nothing while it is closed.
firefox-esr
# The file browser's thumbnails are made by ~/.config/bin/lazyos-thumbnail, and
# for a PDF the first page comes from poppler: 1.1 MB for pdftocairo (plus
# pdftotext and pdftoppm), and it is the only PDF renderer besides Firefox's. No
# XDG thumbnailer package is in this list on purpose - rofi's -preview-cmd runs
# that script instead, and rofi keeps the cache itself
# (~/.cache/thumbnails/normal, 128 px, 3-8 kB a picture).
poppler-utils
# chafa draws a picture, a video frame or an SVG with sixels, which foot renders
# natively, so looking at one costs a foot window (~16 MB measured for a 24 MP
# photo) instead of a browser. 281 kB plus 421 kB of libchafa0t64, and nothing
# resident: the window is the viewer. It was the image viewer until imv arrived
# and is now the *fallback*: imv needs a working EGL context, and a GPU where it
# does not get one (Panfrost is the case this port has hit before) still shows
# the picture in sixels instead of nothing. rofi-files and lazyos-view try imv
# first and fall back here.
chafa
# The image viewer: imv 5.0.1 draws the picture on the GPU (one texture, scaled
# by the renderer), zooms, pans, rotates, plays an animated GIF and steps through
# the directory it was given, so looking at a photo is a viewer and not a
# terminal full of sixels. 375 kB, and it reads PNG/JPEG/GIF/TIFF/WebP/BMP/
# farbfeld plus SVG, HEIF, AVIF and JXL through libraries the image already has
# (librsvg2-2, libheif1, libjxl0.11). Debian installs no /usr/bin/imv - the
# package ships imv-wayland and imv-x11 plus a /usr/libexec/imv/imv wrapper that
# picks one - so scripts/rootfs-setup.sh adds the short name, the same way it
# does for bat and fd. rofi-files, lazyos-view and xdg-open all call `imv`.
imv
# The player: mpv 0.41 decodes anything ffmpeg does (ffmpeg is already in
# packages/tools.list for yt-dlp), seeks, shows subtitles, takes the XF86Audio*
# keys and draws through libplacebo360 (installed) on the Wayland context. 3.5 MB
# plus libass, libsixel1, libbluray/libdvdnav/libcdio, libva and friends. Before
# this, a video container fell through chafa's "unknown file format" to Firefox,
# which cannot play it either; rofi-files and lazyos-view now hand it to mpv,
# detached with setsid so the menu exits instead of waiting for the film.
mpv
# 83 kB, and it installs /etc/mpv/scripts/mpris.so: without it mpv is invisible
# to playerctl, so rofi-media says "nothing is playing" and the bar's player
# module stays empty. It is a plugin mpv loads from its script directory, and
# lazyos-view starts mpv with --no-config (so a profile from another machine
# cannot decide what the session does) which empties those directories - the
# script is therefore passed explicitly as --script=/etc/mpv/scripts/mpris.so.
mpv-mpris
# The document viewer: PDF, DjVu and comics (CBZ/CBR/CB7/CBT), TIFF and
# Illustrator, 7.2 MB plus libppsdocument/libppsview and the GTK4 stack they need
# (libadwaita-1-0, libgtksourceview-5 for annotations, libspelling/libenchant for
# the note spell check, libdjvulibre for DjVu). A reader with search, zoom,
# printing and annotations, where a PDF used to be a 280 MB browser tab. It does
# NOT open EPUB - papers 49's MimeType list has no application/epub+zip, whatever
# docs/omarchy-gap.md said - so an EPUB still goes to the browser, and foliate
# (12.4 MB, in forky) is the package that would fix that if it is wanted.
papers
# The camera application: guvcview 2.2 draws the preview, exposes the device's
# own controls (exposure, gain, white balance - which is the difference between
# a black rectangle and a face in a dim room) and keeps a photo or a clip.
# 514 kB plus libguvcview (496 kB), libportaudio2 (216 kB, so a clip can carry
# the microphone), libwebcam0 (84 kB, the UVC control names) and libgsl (3.3 MB,
# its image filters) - and no new toolkit: it is GTK3, which waybar already
# pulls in, so it inherits the solarized gtk-3.0/gtk.css instead of looking like a
# foreign program. The camera itself needs no package: a USB
# UVC camera is driven by uvcvideo, a module of this kernel, and udev creates
# /dev/video0 when the device (the internal one sits on the hub at 1-1.4) is on
# the bus. ~/.config/bin/lazyos-camera is the wrapper that finds that node by
# driver and starts the window; nothing here is resident.
guvcview
# Mounting removable media as an unprivileged session, which until now was not
# possible at all: udisks2 3.2 MB plus libblockdev (eight modules) and
# libcryptsetup/libnvme, providing /usr/bin/udisksctl and the
# org.freedesktop.UDisks2 service on the system bus. It is D-Bus activated -
# nothing runs until something asks - and it is what the file manager's device
# list and gvfs' volume monitor talk to; rofi-places calls udisksctl to mount and unmount.
# udiskie is deliberately NOT installed: it is a resident Python daemon
# (python3-gi + gir1.2-gtk-3.0 + python3-docopt/python3-yaml, none of them here)
# whose remaining job would be a tray icon this bar does not have. On-demand
# udisksctl in rofi-places is the same result with no daemon.
udisks2
# favnyr's removal action does not run `eject`: it unmounts through
# `udisksctl` (the same tool rofi-places uses) and, when udisks refuses because
# something holds the mount, runs `fuser -m <mountpoint>` to name what does -
# its own string table carries `unmount`, `udisksctl`, `power-off`,
# `--no-user-interaction`, `fuser`, `-m`, and its locale says
# `eject_blocked_by_processes = "In use by: {processes}"`. Without psmisc that
# sentence loses its list, which is the one detail that makes a refused unmount
# fixable. util-linux's `eject` used to be here for pcmanfm and nothing reads it
# any more.
psmisc
# exfatprogs (700 kB, libblkid only) provides mkfs.exfat and fsck.exfat.
# mkfs.exfat is what Format a disk writes, because exFAT has no 4 GB file limit
# and Windows, macOS and Linux all read it; fsck.exfat is what udisks runs on a
# stick before mounting it. dosfstools stays in packages/base.list for the vfat
# sticks udisks also mounts.
exfatprogs
# The file manager is **not a package**: `favnyr` (Brunux/Favnyr-aarm64 v0.3.6,
# 36 MB) is committed at `uis/sway/files/usr/bin/favnyr`, one Slint binary that
# links no GTK and no libgio, so nothing here pulls it in and nothing can
# upgrade it behind the image's back. What stays in this list is what it talks
# to: `gvfs` for the trash and the volume monitor, `libglib2.0-bin` for `gio`'s
# answer to "what opens this file", `udisks2` above for mounting, and `psmisc`
# for naming what holds a disk. Its own settings are `~/.config/favnyr/*.toml`
# (config, openers, favourites, workspace), it takes its icon theme from
# `gtk-3.0/settings.ini`, and `TERMINAL=foot` in `/etc/environment` is what
# makes "Open terminal here" start a terminal, because its fallback list of
# emulators has no foot in it. A double click resolves through
# /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list -> ~/.config/bin/lazyos-view, so it opens what
# rofi-files opens. Why not a GTK file manager: nautilus is a 504-package
# closure, 38 MB of ICU, 31 MB of samba-libs and a localsearch indexer that
# crawls $HOME, and the GTK3 one this image used shipped its own 229 packages
# for a 4.9 MB window (docs/omarchy-gap.md has both measurements).
# gvfs is what gives the window a working Trash (gvfsd-trash) and a local volume
# monitor: 431 kB, plus gvfs-daemons and gvfs-libs. gvfs-backends is deliberately
# not here - it is where samba-libs (31 MB) and the mtp/gphoto/cdio device
# backends come from, so the Network places are simply absent.
gvfs
# `gio` is libglib2.0-bin's CLI, and it is what answers "what opens this file"
# the way the application itself answers it - `gio mime` also proves the
# .desktop entry is accepted, because GIO silently drops one whose Exec program
# does not exist (a lazyos-view without its executable bit reads as "no default
# application", never as an error). check-system.sh asks the same question, and
# without the binary its fallback only reads the file. 0.9 MB, no daemon.
libglib2.0-bin
# foot is the session terminal: no GPU renderer and no Rust runtime, so a window
# costs about half the memory of an alacritty one on a 4 GB machine. The bar's
# popups run here as well (with --app-id=popup, see bar-panels.conf), and
# ~/.config/foot/foot.ini carries the solarized palette.
foot
# (the bar's click-through menus are rofi's dmenu mode now - see `rofi` above and
# ~/.config/bin/bar-pick - so fuzzel is not needed.)
# jq parses `swaymsg -t get_inputs` for the keyboard-layout indicator.
jq
grimshot
grim
slurp
# swappy annotates a screenshot (arrows, text, crop). grimshot only saves or
# copies; Debian has no satty, and swappy is the 179 kB substitute.
swappy
# wf-recorder is screen recording (the capture menu, Alt+Ctrl+C): x264 on the
# CPU, since this SoC has no hardware encoder. It needs ffmpeg, which
# packages/tools.list already installs for yt-dlp.
wf-recorder
# wayvnc serves this session as VNC, so a screen that is misbehaving can be
# looked at from a bigger machine - the other half of the ssh login, which gives
# a shell but not the picture (06-troubleshooting.md, 05-system.md). 0.10.1 is
# 312 kB plus libneatvnc (218 kB) and libaml (82 kB). rofi-vnc starts it on
# 127.0.0.1:5900 with --disable-input (read-only) and stops it again; nothing
# runs until the row in Settings is used. Both halves are decisions: a viewer
# looks and cannot type, and the loopback address means the ssh tunnel is the
# authentication, because wayvnc only authenticates through a certificate and a
# password file (~/.config/wayvnc/config) that this image does not ship.
wayvnc
# wdisplays is the manual display arrangement: a GTK3 window that draws the two
# outputs as rectangles to drag, over wlr-output-management (swayfx advertises
# it; verified here with wlr-randr).  188 kB, and every dependency is already in
# this image.  It is the second half of ~/.config/bin/rofi-display (Settings ->
# Display arrangement), which opens it on a workspace of its own - the
# rectangles need the width - and does the one-line arrangements itself.
# Nothing resident: a window while it is open.
wdisplays
wlsunset
brightnessctl
# pactl(1) for the XF86Audio* keys; pipewire-pulse provides the server only.
pulseaudio-utils

# the idle screen
# It loops the LazyOS wordmark with terminaltexteffects, which Debian ships as
# python3-terminaltexteffects (binary `tte`; see ~/.config/bin/lazyos-screensaver).
# Deboma needs its `ttfx` shim only because Omarchy's scripts call the Rust port
# by that name; this port calls `tte` directly, so no shim is involved.
python3-terminaltexteffects

# notifications
# Debian's package is mako-notifier: the name `mako` belongs to a Python
# template library, and the binary it installs is still /usr/bin/mako. sway
# starts it once per session (etc/sway/config.d/20-notifications.conf) and the
# solarized theme lives in ~/.config/mako/config.
mako-notifier

# login manager
greetd
tuigreet

# Keyboard remapper. The Chromebook's top row is mapped to action keys by the
# device tree and never emits F1-F10, which leaves no way to switch virtual
# terminals; see uis/sway/files/etc/keyd/default.conf and the README.
keyd

# audio
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
# pipewire-alsa routes the ALSA `default` PCM through PipeWire. Without it the
# stock alsa-lib configuration has no usable `default`, so ALSA-only programs
# (aplay/arecord without -D, emulators, some Java/Steam games) fail with
# "audio open error: No such file or directory" while PipeWire clients are fine.
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
# Bluetooth audio support for PipeWire (BlueZ SPA plugins).
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
# ...and the BlueZ daemon they talk to. The SPA plugins are only a client of
# org.bluez over D-Bus: without bluez there is no bluetoothd, no
# bluetoothctl/btmgmt and no bluetooth.service, the controller is never
# powered up and the plugins have nothing to connect to. Debian 5.87 has no
# separate bluez-utils package - bluetoothctl and btmgmt ship in bluez, and
# bluetoothd lives in /usr/libexec/bluetooth/.
bluez
pavucontrol
alsa-ucm-conf

# power / battery
# UPower is what WirePlumber asks for the battery percentage; without it every
# start logs `default: Failed to get percentage from UPower: NameHasNoOwner`.
# waybar's battery module does not need it - it reads /sys/class/power_supply
# directly (its `bat` option takes the device name) - but UPower is the
# component that tracks the battery for everything else, and `upower -d` is the
# way to inspect the fuel gauge.
upower

# graphics (panfrost) + portals
libgl1-mesa-dri
mesa-utils
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
xdg-user-dirs

# fonts / input
fonts-dejavu-core
# The theme is written for "AdwaitaMono Nerd Font" / "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font",
# which Debian does not ship (fonts-adwaita has no Mono variant). JetBrains Mono
# draws the text; the icon glyphs come from fontconfig fallback:
#   * Font Awesome (U+F000-U+F2FF)      -> fonts-font-awesome-legacy
#   * Material Design Icons (plane 15)  -> fonts-materialdesignicons-legacy
#   * Octicons / Codicons / Devicons    -> fonts-nerd-symbols
# The two "legacy" packages are required: Debian's fonts-nerd-symbols is
# DFSG-stripped (no FA, no MDI) and on its own every waybar icon renders as a
# tofu box with the hex codepoint inside. Verified by rendering the icons with
# pango-view, which is what waybar uses.
fonts-jetbrains-mono
fonts-adwaita
fonts-nerd-symbols
fonts-font-awesome-legacy
fonts-materialdesignicons-legacy
# Colour emoji, for the emoji picker (Alt+Ctrl+E). The Nerd Fonts carry
# monochrome symbol planes, not emoji: without this package an inserted emoji
# renders as tofu in the terminal and in waybar, so the picker would work and
# look broken. 10.9 MB installed.
fonts-noto-color-emoji
# Non-Latin text: Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Thai, Armenian, Georgian and the
# rest of Noto's non-CJK set were boxes before this (fc-list :lang=ja/:lang=hi
# and :lang=th answered nothing, and :lang=ar only found four faces).  A file
# name, a web page or an EPUB in one of those scripts is the case this buys.
# fonts-noto-cjk (91 MB) stays out by default and is a documented
# `apt install`; a card that only needs Latin should not pay for it.
fonts-noto-core
playerctl
